# {{packageName}}

V6 extension scaffold. Generate with `redbox-dev-tools init {{hookShortName}} --template v6`.
Install the matching qualified v6 core before `npm run build` and
`npm pack --ignore-scripts`. An identically numbered historical npm core package
may lack the v6 exports. For a built source checkout, use
`npm install --save-dev --save-exact /absolute/path/to/redbox-portal/packages/redbox-core`;
the portal's hook-migration-v6.md gives the complete checkout workflow. Replace
the local reference with the deployment's qualified exact version or complete
packed dependency set before release. Package versions are exact; replace them only with
qualified versions. This template compiles CommonJS and exposes the neutral
`registerRedboxExtension()` contract. It needs no Sails shims or global services.

The application binds each selected factory's concrete input with
`bindExtensionFactory`: `ExampleInput` for the module and `{ example }` for the
handler. Supply an application-owned writable audit path and the finalized
greeting configuration from `config.custom[{{json hookShortName}}]`. The factory
only wires objects; initialize opens the file, activate admits greetings, drain waits for accepted writes and close owns
the native handle. The sample route deliberately permits public access; declare
ordered policy names and supply host policy execution for protected routes.

The migration and bootstrap functions receive `ExampleWorkInput` repository
operations. Registration never runs them. The application's later startup stages
bind/await them after prerequisites; migrations use lexical names and the existing
ledger. `ensureNamedQuery` must implement the deployment's existing upsert policy;
the declaration adds no transaction or exactly-once guarantee. Asset/view paths
are package-relative and included in the artifact.

Legacy `CoreService`, global `sails`, events, `lower` patches and controller shims
need source conversion even when the package stays CommonJS. Install the packed
extension in the portal deployment and supply the concrete module, work and handler bindings through `createPortalHost`. The host owns the
listener and signals. A fresh generated extension is qualified in production
composition, including persisted work, HTTP startup, restart and cleanup, by
`packages/redbox-core/test/qualification/generated-institution.cjs` in the portal
source. Use the deployment's explicit configuration and approved artifacts; this
package does not start a second portal by itself.
